# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored — never commit it.
#
# Hardcover API token, used only by tools/hardcover.mjs, only on your own
# machine, never in the browser. Get one from hardcover.app → account
# settings → Hardcover API. Tokens expire after a year and reset on 1 January.
#
# Treat it like a password: it can read and write your Hardcover account. If
# it ever ends up in a chat window, a screenshot, a commit or a log, generate
# a new one — the old one stays valid until you do.
HARDCOVER_TOKEN=

# Optional: point the tool somewhere else, e.g. the local fixture server.
# HARDCOVER_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:8123

# Google Books API key, used only by tools/describe.mjs, only on your own
# machine. Get one from console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services →
# Credentials → API key, with the Books API enabled. 1,000 free requests/day.
# Without this, describe.mjs skips Google Books cleanly and uses only
# Wikipedia + Wikidata — see DESCRIPTIONS-FEASIBILITY.md for why (this
# sandbox's IP is 429'd by Google Books at any pacing, keyed or not).
GOOGLE_BOOKS_KEY=

# ── Deploying to Vercel (PLAN-PHASE12.md §0/§1.1) ────────────────────────
#
# This file is for your own machine and is never uploaded anywhere — set
# the real values as Vercel PROJECT environment variables instead
# (Project → Settings → Environment Variables), never by committing a
# populated .env.
#
# HARDCOVER_TOKEN — optional on Vercel too. Unset, api/index.js runs on
#   the committed data/cache/*.json + server/fixtures/catalogue.json
#   (vercel.json's `builds[].config.includeFiles` is what ships those into
#   the function), which is the same offline default as local fixture
#   mode — nothing is broken by leaving it unset. Set it if you want live
#   Hardcover lookups from the deployed function. Server-side only, by
#   Vercel's own design: an env var set on the project is never sent to
#   the browser, only read by the function at request time — same
#   guarantee this token already has locally.
#
# GOOGLE_BOOKS_KEY — do NOT set this on Vercel. It is read only by
#   tools/describe.mjs, a local CLI tool that never runs as part of the
#   deployed site, so setting it there does nothing useful and is one
#   more credential sitting somewhere it doesn't need to.
